1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:03,250
I bought a time machine for twelve
dollars.

2
00:00:03,330 --> 00:00:05,750
Now the Roman army wants me dead.

3
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:10,168
And somehow, this is still not the worst
thing I've ever bought online.

4
00:00:10,548 --> 00:00:12,093
Okay. Let me explain.

5
00:00:15,473 --> 00:00:19,711
It started when I found a time machine
online for twelve dollars.

6
00:00:20,291 --> 00:00:21,939
The reviews were amazing.

7
00:00:22,069 --> 00:00:25,466
Five stars. Five stars. Five stars.

8
00:00:25,646 --> 00:00:28,645
And one guy saying it turned his dog into
a refrigerator.

9
00:00:29,225 --> 00:00:30,611
I assumed he was joking.

10
00:00:30,791 --> 00:00:33,098
So I pressed the big red button.

11
00:00:33,678 --> 00:00:35,643
One second, I was in my room.

12
00:00:36,573 --> 00:00:39,061
The next, I was in Ancient Rome.

13
00:00:39,791 --> 00:00:40,905
No Wi-Fi.

14
00:00:41,135 --> 00:00:42,020
No money.

15
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:44,364
No idea what year it was.

16
00:00:44,944 --> 00:00:50,614
My phone had three percent battery, and my
time machine said it needed seven days to recharge.

17
00:00:51,144 --> 00:00:52,882
Seven days.

18
00:00:53,412 --> 00:00:56,480
I couldn't even survive seven minutes
without checking my phone.

19
00:00:57,010 --> 00:00:58,634
Then things got worse.

20
00:00:59,264 --> 00:01:02,468
A Roman soldier saw my phone flashlight.

21
00:01:02,598 --> 00:01:04,018
MAXIMUS: A WIZARD!

22
00:01:04,198 --> 00:01:10,049
And apparently, a tiny glowing rectangle was enough to
convince him I was some kind of powerful wizard.

23
00:01:10,679 --> 00:01:12,747
This was going to be a problem.

24
00:01:14,377 --> 00:01:18,468
By day two, I had discovered something
important about Ancient Rome.

25
00:01:18,698 --> 00:01:20,254
Everybody wanted money.

26
00:01:20,684 --> 00:01:23,024
And nobody accepted Apple Pay.

27
00:01:23,204 --> 00:01:25,670
I tried explaining digital payments.

28
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:29,470
The woman selling bread handed me an
actual apple.

29
00:01:29,550 --> 00:01:31,447
Honestly, fair enough.

30
00:01:32,577 --> 00:01:33,884
Her name was Lucia.

31
00:01:34,064 --> 00:01:36,824
She offered me bread in exchange for
cleaning dishes.

32
00:01:37,004 --> 00:01:40,243
Finally. A job that matched my
qualifications.

33
00:01:40,573 --> 00:01:43,140
I had no qualifications.

34
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:47,156
I even made a friend.

35
00:01:47,336 --> 00:01:50,324
A little kid who found everything I did
hilarious.

36
00:01:50,454 --> 00:01:51,556
Which was nice.

37
00:01:53,786 --> 00:01:57,024
Because back home, people usually just
found it concerning.

38
00:02:00,354 --> 00:02:03,093
On day three, I had a brilliant idea.

39
00:02:03,223 --> 00:02:04,495
I was from the future.

40
00:02:04,575 --> 00:02:06,415
I knew things these people didn't.

41
00:02:06,595 --> 00:02:10,765
So I decided to become rich by introducing
Ancient Rome to modern food.

42
00:02:10,845 --> 00:02:12,822
Specifically, pizza.

43
00:02:13,002 --> 00:02:14,774
There was one small problem.

44
00:02:14,904 --> 00:02:16,109
No tomatoes.

45
00:02:16,839 --> 00:02:19,918
I made something with dough, cheese, and
olives.

46
00:02:20,148 --> 00:02:21,579
Technically edible.

47
00:02:21,859 --> 00:02:23,461
Emotionally devastating.

48
00:02:24,291 --> 00:02:29,131
Lucia took one bite and stared at me like
I'd personally insulted her ancestors.

49
00:02:30,661 --> 00:02:33,286
So that was the end of my restaurant
empire.

50
00:02:35,316 --> 00:02:37,091
Then the soldier found me again.

51
00:02:37,141 --> 00:02:39,342
MAXIMUS: THAT'S HIM! The wizard!

52
00:02:39,472 --> 00:02:40,915
His name was Maximus.

53
00:02:41,145 --> 00:02:45,747
And he had apparently been telling
everyone about the magical glowing rectangle.

54
00:02:46,327 --> 00:02:46,894
Great.

55
00:02:47,524 --> 00:02:49,535
I'd been in Rome for three days.

56
00:02:49,665 --> 00:02:52,869
And I was already involved in a religious
misunderstanding.

57
00:02:55,099 --> 00:02:57,007
MAXIMUS: Show them your power!

58
00:02:57,187 --> 00:03:00,073
Maximus wanted me to demonstrate my
powers.

59
00:03:00,203 --> 00:03:01,316
In public.

60
00:03:01,396 --> 00:03:02,771
For an audience.

61
00:03:03,301 --> 00:03:05,732
Now, normally, I handle pressure very
well.

62
00:03:06,012 --> 00:03:07,103
That was a lie.

63
00:03:07,733 --> 00:03:09,153
My phone was dead.

64
00:03:09,283 --> 00:03:11,646
My time machine still had three days left.

65
00:03:11,826 --> 00:03:13,939
And everyone was staring at me.

66
00:03:16,269 --> 00:03:20,033
I grabbed a shiny tray and reflected
sunlight into his face.

67
00:03:20,083 --> 00:03:20,935
Pure science.

68
00:03:21,065 --> 00:03:22,257
Very advanced.

69
00:03:22,437 --> 00:03:24,982
Mostly because nobody here knew I was
improvising.

70
00:03:25,512 --> 00:03:27,932
Then a pigeon landed on his head.

71
00:03:29,462 --> 00:03:33,757
And for reasons I still don't understand,
everyone started cheering.

72
00:03:34,287 --> 00:03:36,628
Maximus declared me a wizard.

73
00:03:36,758 --> 00:03:40,178
Then he invited me to perform a miracle at
the city festival.

74
00:03:40,308 --> 00:03:41,398
In three days.

75
00:03:41,628 --> 00:03:45,594
I had accidentally become Rome's least
qualified magician.

76
00:03:47,124 --> 00:03:50,294
By day five, people were asking me to
predict the future.

77
00:03:50,424 --> 00:03:51,957
Which sounded easy.

78
00:03:52,087 --> 00:03:54,155
I was literally from the future.

79
00:03:54,385 --> 00:03:57,476
Unfortunately, I knew absolutely nothing
useful.

80
00:03:58,106 --> 00:04:00,798
One man wanted to know whether he'd become
rich.

81
00:04:00,928 --> 00:04:02,746
I told him to invest in property.

82
00:04:03,676 --> 00:04:05,676
Another wanted tomorrow's weather.

83
00:04:05,806 --> 00:04:07,010
I looked at the sky.

84
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:08,094
Not a cloud.

85
00:04:08,324 --> 00:04:10,869
So I confidently predicted sunshine.

86
00:04:11,499 --> 00:04:14,566
For the first time in my life, I felt like
a genius.

87
00:04:14,796 --> 00:04:17,273
That feeling lasted about eleven seconds.

88
00:04:20,303 --> 00:04:21,303
Day six.

89
00:04:21,483 --> 00:04:22,414
It rained.

90
00:04:22,644 --> 00:04:23,485
A lot.

91
00:04:24,115 --> 00:04:28,489
Apparently, people don't enjoy paying for
a weather forecast that's completely wrong.

92
00:04:28,669 --> 00:04:32,032
Especially when the forecast comes from a
wizard.

93
00:04:32,562 --> 00:04:34,585
Maximus was furious.

94
00:04:34,765 --> 00:04:39,151
He said that tomorrow, at the festival, I
would have to prove I could control the weather.

95
00:04:39,281 --> 00:04:40,826
Or I'd be thrown in prison.

96
00:04:41,056 --> 00:04:43,396
This was a slight career setback.

97
00:04:44,126 --> 00:04:45,569
Luckily, I had a plan.

98
00:04:45,699 --> 00:04:47,494
A terrible plan.

99
00:04:47,624 --> 00:04:49,089
But technically a plan.

100
00:04:49,319 --> 00:04:53,978
I'd hide a bucket above the stage, pull a
rope, and make it rain.

101
00:04:54,108 --> 00:04:55,232
Fake rain.

102
00:04:55,362 --> 00:04:56,862
But still rain.

103
00:04:57,592 --> 00:04:59,228
Lucia looked at the machine.

104
00:04:59,308 --> 00:05:00,182
Then at me.

105
00:05:00,262 --> 00:05:01,648
Then back at the machine.

106
00:05:01,878 --> 00:05:03,151
She didn't say anything.

107
00:05:03,381 --> 00:05:06,187
Which, somehow, was worse than yelling.

108
00:05:10,117 --> 00:05:16,423
After six hours of engineering, I had
successfully invented getting hit by a bucket.

109
00:05:18,153 --> 00:05:19,573
Day seven.

110
00:05:19,703 --> 00:05:21,366
The festival.

111
00:05:21,596 --> 00:05:26,925
Hundreds of Romans had gathered to witness
my incredible powers.

112
00:05:27,455 --> 00:05:29,363
I had one bucket.

113
00:05:29,543 --> 00:05:30,718
One rope.

114
00:05:30,898 --> 00:05:34,516
And approximately zero confidence.

115
00:05:35,046 --> 00:05:36,466
The good news?

116
00:05:36,546 --> 00:05:40,409
My time machine had almost finished
recharging.

117
00:05:40,589 --> 00:05:42,008
The bad news?

118
00:05:42,088 --> 00:05:45,951
I needed to survive another ten minutes.

119
00:05:46,181 --> 00:05:50,825
And judging by Maximus's expression, that
was ambitious.

120
00:05:50,955 --> 00:05:53,694
MAXIMUS: Now, wizard. Make it RAIN!

121
00:05:54,124 --> 00:05:56,032
I raised my hands.

122
00:05:56,162 --> 00:05:58,559
I stared into the sky.

123
00:05:58,689 --> 00:06:04,506
I made the mysterious face of a man who
definitely knew what he was doing.

124
00:06:04,836 --> 00:06:06,597
And then...

125
00:06:06,927 --> 00:06:08,102
Nothing.

126
00:06:08,432 --> 00:06:09,852
Not a drop.

127
00:06:12,082 --> 00:06:13,501
Wonderful.

128
00:06:13,731 --> 00:06:18,815
I had officially disappointed an entire
civilization.

129
00:06:19,845 --> 00:06:21,998
Maximus grabbed me.

130
00:06:22,078 --> 00:06:23,986
I grabbed the rope.

131
00:06:24,066 --> 00:06:26,462
The rope grabbed the bucket.

132
00:06:26,542 --> 00:06:29,184
And gravity handled the rest.

133
00:06:29,914 --> 00:06:33,581
For one beautiful second, I made it rain.

134
00:06:33,761 --> 00:06:36,157
On exactly one person.

135
00:06:36,337 --> 00:06:38,978
The angriest man in Ancient Rome.

136
00:06:42,108 --> 00:06:45,238
He did not appreciate the miracle.

137
00:06:45,918 --> 00:06:47,337
So I ran.

138
00:06:49,367 --> 00:06:52,497
I was being chased by Roman soldiers.

139
00:06:54,027 --> 00:06:56,424
My shoes were falling apart.

140
00:06:56,754 --> 00:07:01,105
And the world's cheapest time machine was
finally waking up.

141
00:07:01,235 --> 00:07:03,387
There was just one problem.

142
00:07:03,617 --> 00:07:06,014
Maximus had taken it.

143
00:07:06,744 --> 00:07:10,118
Then something incredible happened.

144
00:07:10,298 --> 00:07:13,672
Remember that kid I shared my bread with?

145
00:07:13,902 --> 00:07:20,745
Apparently, one piece of bread was enough to
buy the best emergency rescue service in Rome.

146
00:07:20,875 --> 00:07:21,786
BOY: Catch!

147
00:07:21,836 --> 00:07:25,210
He grabbed my watch and threw it back to
me.

148
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:30,718
For the first time all week, a decision
I'd made had actually worked.

149
00:07:31,248 --> 00:07:32,667
Three seconds.

150
00:07:32,947 --> 00:07:33,878
Two.

151
00:07:34,158 --> 00:07:35,089
One.

152
00:07:35,519 --> 00:07:37,651
I pressed the button.

153
00:07:37,701 --> 00:07:39,833
The portal opened.

154
00:07:39,883 --> 00:07:41,233
I jumped.

155
00:07:41,283 --> 00:07:43,924
And Maximus grabbed my hoodie.

156
00:07:47,804 --> 00:07:51,178
I survived seven days in Ancient Rome.

157
00:07:51,358 --> 00:07:54,243
I escaped an entire army.

158
00:07:54,573 --> 00:07:56,823
And somehow...

159
00:07:57,353 --> 00:07:59,750
I brought the army with me.

160
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:04,776
My twelve-dollar time machine had turned
into a group booking.

161
00:08:09,406 --> 00:08:11,308
I should've read the reviews.
